/T (Transformer models are encoder architectures based on the self-attention mechanism, which allows each word to be represented in the context of all other words in the input sequence. This contextual representation is far richer than traditional bag-of-words or TF-IDF approaches, which treat words independently. Models such as BERT, RoBERTa, and DistilBERT are first pretrained on massive unlabeled text corpora to acquire broad linguistic knowledge.)
/E (Transformer models are encoder architectures based on the self-attention mechanism, which allows each word to be represented in the context of all other words in the input sequence. This contextual representation is far richer than traditional bag-of-words or TF-IDF approaches, which treat words independently. Models such as BERT, RoBERTa, and DistilBERT are first pretrained on massive unlabeled text corpora to acquire broad linguistic knowledge.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
69 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 39 0 R
/T (HateXplain is a publicly available dataset for hate speech analysis hosted on Hugging Face \(Hate-speech-CNERG/hatexplain\). It contains 19,229 social media posts from Gab and Twitter, each annotated by three human annotators with one of three labels: hateful \(0\), normal \(1\), or offensive \(2\). The final label is determined by majority vote. The dataset provides official train/validation/test splits. The class distribution in the training set is: normal \(40.64%\), hateful \(30.87%\), and offensive \(28.50%\). This moderate imbalance was handled through class-weighted loss functions in transformer experiments and class-weighted Logistic Regression in the baseline. Text lengths range from 2 to 165 words \(median: 21 words\), guiding the choice of max_length=128 tokens.)
/E (HateXplain is a publicly available dataset for hate speech analysis hosted on Hugging Face \(Hate-speech-CNERG/hatexplain\). It contains 19,229 social media posts from Gab and Twitter, each annotated by three human annotators with one of three labels: hateful \(0\), normal \(1\), or offensive \(2\). The final label is determined by majority vote. The dataset provides official train/validation/test splits. The class distribution in the training set is: normal \(40.64%\), hateful \(30.87%\), and offensive \(28.50%\). This moderate imbalance was handled through class-weighted loss functions in transformer experiments and class-weighted Logistic Regression in the baseline. Text lengths range from 2 to 165 words \(median: 21 words\), guiding the choice of max_length=128 tokens.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
